  "worked_solution": "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)\\ne \\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''.",
